description:
build_with_aia in pkix-chain/src/lib.rs lines 655-679:

    let uri_refs: Vec<&str> = new_uris.iter().map(String::as_str).collect();
    let results = self.aia.batch_fetch(&uri_refs);

    let mut added_any = false;
    for (uri, result) in new_uris.iter().zip(results) {
        ...
    }

The AiaFetcher trait's batch_fetch contract says (pkix-aia/src/lib.rs:570-572): 'Returns a Vec aligned with uris by index: the i-th entry is the result for uris[i].' But the trait is implementer-overridable and the default impl just maps fetch() over the slice. A buggy or adversarial implementation could return a Vec of the wrong length.

If batch_fetch returns FEWER results than uris (e.g. the impl 'returns early' on first failure), .zip silently truncates: trailing URIs are dropped without any error. Their potential responses are never tried, last_aia_error reflects whatever the SHORTER prefix produced, and added_any may be false despite the truncation. The caller sees Error::Aia(...) when in fact the chain could have validated if the dropped URIs had been tried.

If batch_fetch returns MORE results than uris (less plausible but still a contract violation), the extras are silently ignored — less critical.

SCENARIO that triggers the cost: a contributor writes a custom AiaFetcher impl that does HTTP/2 multiplexing and accidentally returns early when one of N parallel fetches fails (closing the connection drops the others). build_with_aia silently treats the dropped URIs as 'we already tried them' (because tried_uris.insert() happened upfront, before batch_fetch was called) — so they are NEVER retried in subsequent iterations either. Permanent silent loss.

MAINTENANCE CONSEQUENCE: A future bug report 'verify_one randomly fails on multi-AIA-URI chains under load' is impossible to root-cause from the current code. There is no assertion that results.len() == uri_refs.len(); there is no panic; there is no error variant.

FIX: replace .zip with an explicit length check. Either:

    if results.len() != uri_refs.len() {
        return Err(Error::Aia(/* new variant: BatchFetcherContractViolation */));
    }

…or document the contract violation as an undefined behaviour and add a debug_assert!.

RECOMMENDATION: a debug_assert!(results.len() == uri_refs.len(), ...) is the cheapest defensive guard. Tests run with debug_assertions on; a buggy fetcher impl trips on its first batch call rather than silently dropping URIs.
